    "body": "## Problem\nIf two staged events for the same `(tenant_id, legacy_id)` land in one dbt batch, `person_unified.sql` (incremental) hits\n`person_tenant_id_legacy_id_key` (\"duplicate key value violates unique constraint\"). The batch is never marked processed,\nso every later `dbt run` retries the same batch and fails: the pipeline is wedged until someone intervenes. Already known\nfrom #4 (the `poisons_stack` xfails); this ticket tracks the fix.\n\n## New evidence (found while working #12)\n`make test-resilience` on branch `issue-12-offset-commit-after-failed-stage`: `test_offset_replay__verify` fails. After the\noffset reset the consumer replays all history; `tenant_a1` id 5 appears twice in Kafka (staged at 11:04:07 and 11:04:24\nin the preceding `legacy_down` scenario, probably Debezium re-emitting the row after the legacy DB restarted), so both\nevents are in one batch. Postgres logs the duplicate key error, `dbt transformation failed:` follows (blank, see below),\nand the canary row never reaches `person`. So a normal offset replay can trigger it, not just rapid updates.\n\n## Suggested fix\nIn the model, reduce each batch to the latest staged event per `(tenant_id, source_id)` (e.g. `row_number() over\n(partition by tenant_id, source_id order by id desc) = 1`) before the incremental merge/insert. Check DELETE handling at\nthe same time (DELETE events are currently dead-lettered, see #12), since \"latest event wins\" must not resurrect a deleted row.\n\nSmall related improvement: `run_dbt_transformation` prints `result.stderr`, but dbt writes errors to stdout, so failures are\nlogged blank. Log the tail of stdout too.\n\n## Done when\n- [ ] Two events for one key in one batch no longer fail `dbt run` (dbt model test in `tests/dbt`).\n- [ ] The `poisons_stack` xfails in `tests/e2e/test_edge_cases.py` that this fixes are removed (strict xfail will tell us which).\n- [ ] `test_offset_replay__verify` passes on a stack that has had the legacy-down scenario run before it.\n\nRelated: #4, #12.\n",
